// AppInstructions is shared product policy, independent of a project's framework.
const AppInstructions = `Runtime contract for backend applications:
Write ai-os.app.json at the workspace root using version 1, for example:
{"version":1,"database":"postgres","build":[{"cwd":"api","command":["go","build","-o","server","."]}],"services":[{"name":"api","cwd":"api","command":["./server"],"route":"/api","healthPath":"/api/health"},{"name":"web","cwd":"web","command":["node","server.js"],"route":"/","healthPath":"/"}],"checks":[{"command":["python3","tests/acceptance.py"]}]}
Use only the services and steps needed. Commands are argument arrays, not implicit shell strings. Cwd is workspace-relative. Supported toolchains are installed system binaries or project-local executables; never require host config or a Docker socket. One root route / is required. Route prefixes are preserved (/api/x reaches the API as /api/x). The host supplies HOST=127.0.0.1 and a distinct PORT to each service, NAME_URL for each service, APP_URL for the root service, and DATABASE_URL when PostgreSQL is requested. Bind HOST and PORT; never hardcode ports or credentials. Serve a usable UI at the root service. Checks must use these URLs, exit nonzero on failure, test requested behavior and validation, and clean up only their own test records. Build steps run before services; checks run after every service is HTTP-ready. Readiness is not proof of correctness.
Declare database:postgres when a managed database is needed; the host provisions it on Build & start app. Preserve existing data with migrations. If no DATABASE_URL exists during coding, report integration checks as pending runtime verification; do not silently replace a requested managed database with a different store. Static HTML needs no runtime manifest. Report exactly what was tested, and tell the user to use Build & start app for backend applications. Do not claim a completed coding job is a deployed or verified application.

`
